The [mode of a model of normals](../nonlinear-least-squares/) minimizes a sum of squared residuals and log-volumes.
[Newton's method](../trust-regions/) would minimize it with the function's whole curvature, which needs the second derivatives of every residual.
Almost every method that estimates such a model does something cheaper: it replaces each residual by its first-order expansion at a point, and is left with a quadratic model whose curvature needs first derivatives alone.
This note defines that replacement, says exactly what it keeps of the function and what it leaves out, reads it as a model in a trust region, and proves what the iteration built on it converges to.
The note after it reads the same replacement as a Gaussian.

## The setting

<!-- The anchor of the section that defined the objective, which moved to the note on models of
     normals as least squares and which a document of the archive still links to. -->
<span id="a-sum-of-squares-and-what-else"></span>

The function to be linearized is the objective [](eq:math/nonlinear-least-squares/objective), a sum over terms $t$ of half a squared whitened residual $r_t$ and a log-volume $\lambda_t$, on a product of manifolds with retractions with the stacked tangent $\R^\dimension$.
Each term reads only its variables $K_t$.
Write $g$ for the [chart gradient](../derivatives-in-charts/) of $f$ at the point of linearization $\hat \point$.
Every derivative below is in the charts at $\hat \point$, so that on a curved factor it is the derivative of a function of $\hat \point \oplus \tangent$ at $\tangent = 0$.

## The linearization

<Statement {...linearization} />

The linearization agrees with $f$ to first order, and its curvature is the *Gauss–Newton matrix*:

$$
	m(0) = f(\hat \point), \qquad \nabla m(0) = g, \qquad \nabla^2 m = \sum_t J_t\transpose J_t . @tag(agreement)
$$

The first holds by the choice of $c_0$.
For the second, the gradient of $f$ is $g = \sum_t J_t\transpose r_t + \sum_t \nabla \lambda_t$, while the gradient of $m$ at $\tangent = 0$ is $\sum_t J_t\transpose (r_t + \hat c_t) + \eta$.
For a folded term, $\D r_t = W_t \D s_t$, so $\nabla \lambda_t = \D s_t\transpose \nabla \lambda_t(s_t) = J_t\transpose W_t^{-\top} \nabla \lambda_t(s_t) = J_t\transpose \hat c_t$, and the two gradients agree term by term.

**Folding a log-volume into its rows.**
A folded log-volume's gradient lies in the row space of its own term's rows, so it is carried by the right-hand side, $-(r_t + \hat c_t)$, and not by the linear term.
The normal's log-volume is folded on the Euclidean spaces, where it is zero, and on the curved ones, where it is a function of $s_t = \point \ominus \mu$ alone: of $s_t$ itself on a group whose chart translates the identity's, and of $\lvert s_t \rvert$ on the sphere with orthonormal bases.
On a positive space it is $\sum_i \log \point_i$, which depends on the point and not only on $s_t$, and it stays in $\eta$.
So a model of Euclidean and curved quantities has $\eta = 0$ exactly.
Folding costs nothing, since $\hat c_t$ is a gradient of the size of $s_t$; why it is worth doing, and why $\eta$ is read off the unfolded log-volumes rather than computed as a difference of gradients, are questions of how the model is solved, which [the normal equations](../normal-equations/#a-linear-term-and-a-fold) answer.

Each term's rows $J_t$ are nonzero only in the columns of its variables $K_t$, and each piece of the linear term is the gradient of one log-volume, nonzero only on the variables of its own term.
So the linearization is a sum of pieces, one for each term, each a function of a few blocks of $\tangent$, with the structure of the terms alone.

## What is left out

The Hessian of $\tangent \mapsto f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)$ at $\tangent = 0$, which at a stationary point is the chart Hessian of [%full](statement:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian), is

$$
	\nabla^2 f = \sum_t J_t\transpose J_t + \sum_t \sum_i (r_t)_i\, \nabla^2 (r_t)_i + \sum_t \nabla^2 \lambda_t . @tag(hessian)
$$

The linearization keeps the first sum.
The second is what every Gauss–Newton method omits: it is small where the residuals are, and it is what makes the Hessian indefinite far from a minimum.
The third is the curvature of the log-volumes: zero on the Euclidean spaces, zero on the positive ones, whose log-volume is linear in their chart, and on a curved space of the relative order of the noise's variance.
The right-hand sides and the linear term keep the log-volumes' gradients exactly.

When the rows determine every direction, the minimizer of the linearization is the *Gauss–Newton step*

$$
	\tangent^* = -\Bigl(\sum_t J_t\transpose J_t\Bigr)^{-1} g , @tag(step)
$$

computed from the rows and never from this formula, for reasons that [the normal equations](../normal-equations/) give.
The step returns to the manifold by the retraction, $\hat \point \oplus \tangent^*$, factor by factor.
A factor whose rows do not determine it, such as a landmark seen from one place along one direction, has no step: the direction is the model's to fix, by a prior or by a damping, and not the linear algebra's to guess.

## A model in a trust region

The linearization is a quadratic model [](eq:math/trust-regions/model) of $f$ at $\hat \point$: it has the gradient $g$ and the curvature $M = \sum_t J_t\transpose J_t$, positive semidefinite by construction.
Minimizing it is the step [](eq:math/trust-regions/step) of that curvature, and the Gauss–Newton step is a quasi-Newton step whose curvature leaves out the last two sums of [](eq:hessian).

Like every such model it is good only near $\hat \point$, and the [trust region](../trust-regions/#the-trust-region) applies to it unchanged.
By [%full](statement:math/trust-regions/trust-region-step), the step within a radius solves

$$
	\Bigl( \sum_t J_t\transpose J_t + \lambda D \Bigr) \tangent = -g @tag(damped)
$$

for a damping $\lambda \ge 0$, and every damped step is the trust-region step of some radius.
The Gauss–Newton step is the trust-region step with the radius infinite, $\lambda = 0$.
It is not itself a trust-region method: alone it trusts the model everywhere, and from a poor start it overshoots.
The method that chooses $\lambda$ by the gain ratio [](eq:math/trust-regions/gain) of each step, the Levenberg–Marquardt method, is the trust-region method on this model, and a later note states its choices.

## What the iteration converges to

Repeating the step is the Gauss–Newton method.
It uses the gradient of all of $f$ and the curvature of the squares alone, and the next statement says that the first decides where it stops and the second only how fast it gets there.

<Statement {...gaussNewton} />

<Proof>

This is [%full](statement:math/trust-regions/curvature-iteration) with $M = J\transpose J$, whose rate is the spectral radius of $I - (J\transpose J)^{-1} H^\ast = -(J\transpose J)^{-1} E$.

</Proof>

With $s = \sum_t \lambda_t$, this is the iteration of the linearization, and three things follow.

- **The answer depends on the gradient alone.**
  Whatever matrix stands on the left, the iteration stops where $g = 0$.
  An iteration that includes the log-volumes' gradients converges to a stationary point of $f$ itself, the mode of the model's density; one that leaves them out converges to the minimum of the squares alone, which is [the mode of another model](../normal/#the-gaussian-part-alone).
  The sparsity of $J\transpose J$ is the same either way, since each log-volume depends on the variables its own residual already reads.
- **Leaving the log-volumes' Hessian out costs little.**
  Write $t_i$ for the tangent coordinate $\point \ominus \mu$ of the $i$-th term, $A_i = \D t_i$, and $\Lambda_i$ and $\Sigma_i$ for its precision and covariance.
  In $E$ the log-volumes contribute $\D^2 s = \sum_i A_i\transpose \nabla^2 \lambda_i\, A_i$, besides terms in $\nabla \lambda_i$ that vanish at $t_i = 0$, while $J\transpose J = \sum_i A_i\transpose \Lambda_i A_i$.
  If $-\kappa \Lambda_i \preceq \nabla^2 \lambda_i \preceq \kappa \Lambda_i$ for every term, then $-\kappa J\transpose J \preceq \D^2 s \preceq \kappa J\transpose J$, and the log-volumes add at most $\kappa$ to $\rho$, with $\kappa = \max_i \lvert \Sigma_i^{1/2} \nabla^2 \lambda_i\, \Sigma_i^{1/2} \rvert$.
  Near $t = 0$ that is $\sigma^2$ for the Cayley and stereographic charts, with $\sigma^2$ the largest variance of a rotation's or a direction's noise: $0.01$ per iteration at $\sigma = 0.1$ rad.
  The rest of $E$ is the residuals' curvature, which on a curved space includes the second derivatives of $\ominus$, of the same relative order $\sigma^2$.
  The log-volume does not change the order of the approximation, and a positive factor adds nothing at all to $E$, its log-volume being linear in its chart.
- **Adding it is safe where a Gaussian approximation is.**
  When $\kappa < 1$, $J\transpose J + \D^2 s \succeq (1 - \kappa)\, J\transpose J$ is positive definite, so it may stand on the left instead.
  The condition $\kappa < 1$ is, term by term, the one under which [a normal's mean is a mode at all](../normal/#what-the-parameters-mean), $\sigma < 1$ in the chart's coordinates.
  Where it fails, the density is not concentrated around one mode, and no method of this kind fits it.

## Where it is exact

<Statement {...exact} />

<Proof>

On a space flat in disguise the chart at $\hat \point$ and the chart at any other point differ by a translation, by [](eq:math/normal/translation), so a function affine in one chart's coordinates is affine in every chart's, and the hypothesis does not depend on $\hat \point$.
An affine residual has $r_t(\hat \point \oplus \tangent) = r_t + J_t \tangent$ exactly, and an affine log-volume is equal to its first-order expansion, so $f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent) = \sum_t \tfrac12 \lvert r_t + J_t \tangent \rvert^2 + \sum_t \lambda_t(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)$, plus the constant, is $m(\tangent)$.

</Proof>

The normal on a Euclidean space with a fixed scale and a mean affine in the variables is such a term, and so is the normal on a positive space, the log-normal, with a mean whose logarithm is affine in their logarithms.
A model of such terms is *linear-Gaussian*, and on it one Gauss–Newton step from anywhere lands on the mode.

## Where it fails

The linearization rests on the normalizers being constant, and so on the scales being fixed ([where the form breaks](../nonlinear-least-squares/#where-the-form-breaks)).
Carrying a free scale's normalizer's gradient in $\eta$ would not mend it.
The residual depends on the scale, and the residual's own curvature in the scale's coordinates, which the rows omit, is as large as what they keep.
For an isotropic scale in its logarithmic chart $u = \log \sigma$, $r = e^{-u} \varepsilon$, so the rows' curvature in $u$ is $\lvert r \rvert^2$ and the squares' is $2 \lvert r \rvert^2$, while the normalizer $-\dimension u$ is linear.
At the stationary point $\lvert r \rvert^2 = \dimension$, the omitted part equals the kept part, and the rate of [%full](statement:gauss-newton) is one: the iteration need not converge.
A scale is estimated with the expected information in the Hessian's place, by [Fisher scoring](../fisher-scoring/).
